.SS Abstract
This header provides a minimal, portable abstraction for far\-pointer
support across compilers and platforms used to access memory locations
beyond the current segment in \f[B]16\-bit architectures\f[R].
.PP
In 16\-bit compilers (MS\-DOS, Turbo C, Watcom, etc.), pointers are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Near\f[R]: within a single segment
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Far\f[R]: across segments
.PP
Memory segmentation in 16\-bit systems uses 64KB segments:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Near pointer\f[R] \-> 16\-bit offset
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Far pointer\f[R] \-> 32\-bit (segment:offset)
.PP
It defines the \f[CR]FAR\f[R] macro to annotate far memory qualifiers
where supported, and the \f[CR]IS__FAR__SUPPORTED\f[R] macro to allow
compile\-time detection of far\-pointer availability.
.PP
When far pointers are not supported, the macros safely degrade to
no\-ops, preserving source compatibility without impacting behavior.
.PP
Note: On classic 16\-bit segmented systems (e.g.
real\-mode x86), the theoretical physical address space is up to 1 MB
(20\-bit addressing via segment:offset).
Far pointers allow access outside the current segment but do not
increase object size limits.
The practically usable memory is often much smaller and depends on the
CPU mode, memory model, and compiler/runtime support (EMS/XMS, huge
pointers, etc.).
Always consult and trust your compiler and target hardware
documentation.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Pro\-Tip\f[R]:
.IP \(bu 2
Check your compiler documentation for memory models and pointer types.
.IP \(bu 2
On \f[B]modern systems\f[R] (32/64\-bit), far pointers are obsolete and
mostly ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
Far pointers are slower (due to segment switching).
.IP \(bu 2
Use \f[CR]FAR\f[R] only if you\(aqre planning to compile your code on
16\-bit real mode environments.
.RE
