   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR
.IP
.EX
#define FAR
.EE
.PP
The memory access type depending on the compiler and platform.
.IP \(bu 2
On \f[B]16\-bit systems\f[R], it expands to a far pointer keyword (e.g.,
\f[CR]far\f[R], \f[CR]_far\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
On \f[B]modern systems\f[R], it\(aqs often empty or unused.
.PP
Since GNU does not support the \f[CR]far\f[R] keyword (the
\f[CR]__attribute__((far))\f[R] extension is excluded, as it works
differently), this keyword is defined as empty in GNU.
.PP
\f[B]Examples\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
char FAR *far_ptr = far_malloc(128000);
.EE
.PP
For function pointers:
.IP
.EX
void (FAR *far_function)();
.EE
.PP
For type casting:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// \(dqvariable\(dq and \(dqfunction\(dq must refer to FAR\-addressable storage\f[R]

void FAR *test1 = (void FAR *)variable;
void (FAR *test2)() = (void (FAR *)())function;
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS IS__FAR__SUPPORTED
.IP
.EX
#define IS__FAR__SUPPORTED
.EE
.PP
Checks whether the \f[CR]FAR\f[R] macro is defined as non\-empty.
This indicates that the compiler truly supports the \f[CR]far\f[R]
keyword.
.RS
.PP
Note:
.PP
Checking \f[CR]IS__FAR__SUPPORTED\f[R] alone is not sufficient.
.PP
You must also verify that your target architecture and compiler runtime
support far memory allocation and memory access routines compatible with
far pointers.
.RE
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
#ifdef IS__FAR__SUPPORTED
void FAR *  test = far_malloc(128000);
\&...
#else
#ifdef __SYSTEM_16_BIT__
\f[I]// You must really gotta do something in here\f[R]
\&...
#else \f[I]// No trouble at all (hope)\f[R]
void *test = malloc(128000);
\&...
#endif
#endif
.EE
